y morphism\n<MATH_0010> the map <MATH_0011>\nis called the <TEXTITOPEN_0003>restriction map<TEXTITCLOSE_0003> and is often denoted\n<MATH_0012>. Another way\nof expressing this is to say that <MATH_0013> is the <TEXTITOPEN_0004>pullback<TEXTITCLOSE_0004>\nof <MATH_0014> via <MATH_0015>. Functoriality means that <MATH_0016>.\nSometimes we use the notation <MATH_0017>.\nThis notation is consistent with the notion of restriction\nof functions from topology because if <MATH_0018>\nare morphisms in <MATH_0019> and <MATH_0020> is a section of\n<MATH_0021> over <MATH_0022> then <MATH_0023> by the\nfunctorial nature of <MATH_0024>. Of course we have to be\ncareful since it may very well happen\nthat there is more than one morphism <MATH_0025> and it is\ncertainly not going to be the case that the corresponding\npullback maps are equal.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:14237cef221a0e01d36ad3e866a34ff6aa8d4057d3742b42c4a2e9a1c057df02", "unit_id": "tag:00V1:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "00V1", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0003": "$\\textit{Sets}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\textit{PSh}(\\mathcal{C})$", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0002": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it ", "TEXTITOPEN_0002": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-presheaves-sets}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:9447a3c625161e4a176edcf46e290368fe9022e4909d2f2ff52a2dfe91a6122e", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:2dee885e5c26c4909a993b5070060db5238f66204e1d37316868152a21ead5e2", "source_text": "A <TEXTITOPEN_0001>presheaf of sets<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> on <MATH_0001> is a contravariant\nfunctor from <MATH_0002> to <MATH_0003>. <TEXTITOPEN_0002>Morphisms\nof presheaves<TEXTITCLOSE_0002> are transformations of functors. The category\nof presheaves of sets is denoted <MATH_0004>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b0283045f01084b9a6e3f3f54b6b33b937576f5bec94ce268c39e5598aaee36e", "unit_id": "tag:00V2:statement"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "00V1", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$U$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0003": "$h_U$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0005": "\\begin{equation}\n\\label{equation-map-representable-into-presheaf}\n\\Mor_{\\textit{PSh}(\\mathcal{C})}(h_U, \\mathcal{F})\n=\n\\mathcal{F}(U).\n\\end{equation}", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-example-hom-functor}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{categories-lemma-yoneda}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:d76fe92aa1935786cabba2309adf153b2bcbb2338b14358f07349550e6488504", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e32a876384b2566e2f3661122ad0f10151830775cafb2e834b10c338b49f8100", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nNote that for any object <MATH_0001> of <MATH_0002> the functor of\npoints <MATH_0003>, see Categories, Example <REF_0001>\nis a presheaf. These are called the <TEXTITOPEN_0001>representable presheaves<TEXTITCLOSE_0001>.\nThese presheaves have the pleasing property that for any\npresheaf <MATH_0004> we have\n<MATH_0005>\nThis is the Yoneda lemma (Categories, Lemma <REF_0002>).\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nSimilarly, we can define the notion of a presheaf of abelian groups,\nrings, etc. More generally we may define a presheaf with values in a\ncategory.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:9eeb6106e19d1725a91a1f5b430de472b2f6f1d7e4baff4290c4551bffbec525", "unit_id": "tag:00V1:prose-0002"}
